\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Fixed length and a fluctuating radius}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For fixed-length sign matter the hopping factor in a scalar
frame is a function of one link. This is the unitary-gauge
form of the sign model in
\citet{authors’ preprint, §II, pp. 17–19, (2.15)}{fradkin1979higgs}.
We cite their infinite-volume analyticity result for the fixed-length
models and couplings covered by their cluster expansion. The
plaquette coupling can be sufficiently small in the strong
gauge-coupling regime, and the hopping coupling sufficiently
large relative to the plaquette coupling in the Higgs regime.
On a connected domain joining those regimes the free energy
and expectations of local gauge-invariant observables are analytic
in the two couplings (Appendix, pp. 43–47).}

\p{The angular field in that argument can be eliminated completely.
The charge-one circle field has this property. In the #{SU(N)}
example the authors use group-valued matrices with the fundamental
trace hopping and plaquette terms (§IV, pp. 35–37, (4.1)).
For #{N>2} a single unit vector in the defining representation
has a nontrivial stabilizer. Likewise a circle scalar of integer
charge greater than one has a residual finite subgroup. The
connected-domain conclusion uses the hypotheses of the cited
expansion.}

\p{\citet{§4, pp. 456–460, (4.1)–(4.8)}{osterwalder1978gauge}
give each scalar a radius and a group-valued angle. Their examples
are a complex scalar for #{U(1)} and a complex doublet for #{SU(2)}.
The change to reduced links eliminates the angles from the action,
while the hopping still contains both endpoint radii. We cite
their separate infinite-volume result (Theorem 4.1, p. 460).
For each positive gauge coupling and positive hopping coupling
there exist a positive radial minimum location and positive
quadratic bounds defining an admissible class of radial potentials.
The lower quadratic bound holds at every radius and the upper
one near the minimum, as in (4.2). For potentials in this class
the cluster expansion converges uniformly in the finite volume.
Local observables have limiting expectations with physical
positivity, exponential clustering and lattice translation
invariance.}
